<h2>Basics of Radix Sort</h2>
<p>
    Radix Sort works by looping through the array multiple times, 
    sorting elements based on the value of a certain digit each loop.
    <br>
    <br>
    For example, let's look at the number <b>1234</b>.
    The least significant digit of <b>1234</b> is 4.
    Then, 3 would be the next least significant digit, followed by 2, 
    and finally 1 would be the most significant digit.
    <br>
    <br>
    Let's say we have the array <b>[1234, 4321, 2134]</b>, 
    and that we wanted to sort it starting from the least significant digit.
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                The first pass, which will sort the array on the least significant digit, the ones place, 
                will sort the array into <b>[4321, 1234, 2134]</b>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Then, the second pass will sort the array based on the tens palce,
                which results in <b>[4321, 1234, 2134]</b>. Notice how <b>1234</b> and <b>2134</b> are in the same order as they were before.
                Radix Sort is a stable algorithm, so elements with the same value will be in the same order.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Then, the third pass will sort the array based on the hundreds place,
                which results in <b>[2134, 1234, 4321]</b>.
            </p>
            
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Finally, the third pass will sort the array based on the thounsands place, 
                which is the most significant digit for this array.
                The final pass results in <b>[1234, 2134, 4321]</b>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</p>